# SQL's uppercase `WHERE`: DS0105, with a fix to `where`.
mode: syntax
---- query
'orders.csv' | WHERE amount > 10
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..33
  PIPELINE@0..32
    LITERAL@0..12
      STRING@0..12 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@12..13 " "
    PIPE@13..14 "|"
    WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
    WHERE_OP@15..32
      IDENT@15..20 "WHERE"
      WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
      BIN_EXPR@21..32
        NAME_REF@21..27
          IDENT@21..27 "amount"
        WHITESPACE@27..28 " "
        GT@28..29 ">"
        WHITESPACE@29..30 " "
        LITERAL@30..32
          LONG@30..32 "10"
  WHITESPACE@32..33 "\n"

error[DS0105]: SQL keyword `WHERE` is not an operator
  --> query:1:16
  |
1 | 'orders.csv' | WHERE amount > 10
  |                ^^^^^ this is SQL
  |
  = help: operator names are lowercase: write `where`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    'orders.csv' | where amount > 10
which parses without errors
